  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe front lit up with convection overnight along the MI/IN border \nand with the help of a remnant convectively forced meso-low looks \nlike it will continue its upscale growth as it tracks south and west \nof SE MI toward morning. There's still a chance Lenawee and Monroe \ncan get clipped by northern reaches of the convective cluster as the \nlow tried to track eastward just south of the border, but this \ncurrent convection will force the more potent instability axis \nsouthwest of the area, making it harder to sustain itself into the \nCWA. Will keep a chance of thunderstorms mainly south of I94 through \nthe early morning to account for this potential, whether we get \nclipped by the strong storms or more of elevated convection further \nup the frontal slope utilizing the 1000 J/kg MUCAPE. Other area to \nwatch will be closer to the I69 corridor where there is another \nconvergence axis on the north edge of the shear with elevated cape \nin the area as well. CAMs have been trying to generate some \nscattered showers for the several cycles and current regional radar \nshows a few showers trying to get going so will carry a low end pop \nthrough the late morning to account for potential activity carrying \non until drier air under the sinking high pressure system can end \nthe showers for the rest of the weekend. Severe weather is not \nanticipated due to elevated nature of any storms that develop. \n\nHeights then build through the day as the amplified ridge over the \nPlains drifts eastward over the Great Lakes. At the same time, a \nretrograding upper low over the east coast of Canada will force a \nsurface high south and west over the Great Lakes bringing a less \nhumid and cooler airmass. Dewpoints will hover in the upper 50s to \nlow 60s with highs in the upper 70s Saturday though Monday. Next \nchance of precip arrives Wednesday as a strong Pacific wave flattens \nthe longwave ridge after pushing the ridge axis across the state. \nThe associated surface low well to the north over Ontario sends a \ncold front down through the Great Lakes. Warm advection ahead of the \nfront Tuesday will raise max temps back into the low 80s with mid to \nupper 80s on Wednesday before the fropa. This front will again bring \na cooler airmass with highs dropping into the mid 70s for Thursday.",
